<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bishops' Wars</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1639">1639</a>–<a href="/years/1640">1640</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-the-three-kingdoms">Wars of the Three Kingdoms</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishops</a>' Wars were two brief conflicts in 1639 and 1640 between Charles I and the Scottish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/covenanters">Covenanters</a>, who rejected his imposition of bishops and a new prayer book on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-scotland">Church of Scotland</a>. The Scots defeated the English at Newburn and occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newcastle-upon-tyne">Newcastle</a> in 1640. Charles had to recall Parliament to pay for the war, opening the way to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-civil-war">English Civil War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bishops-wars.jpg" alt="Bishops' Wars" width="320" height="199" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: William Allan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Signing_of_the_National_Covenant_in_Greyfriars_Kirkyard,_Edinburgh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-scotland">Church of Scotland</a>, the Kirk, was Presbyterian in structure and Calvinist in doctrine. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-vi-and-i">James VI and I</a> regarded a unified church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> as the first step towards a centralised state, a policy his son Charles I adopted, although many in both kingdoms thought his reforms of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a> essentially Catholic.</p><p>In 1636 a new Book of Canons excommunicated anyone who denied the king's supremacy in church matters, and in 1637 William Laud's Scottish Book of Common Prayer caused anger and rioting, said to have been set off by Jenny Geddes throwing a stool in St Giles Cathedral. In February 1638 delegates agreed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-covenant">National Covenant</a>, and the General Assembly meeting at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glasgow">Glasgow</a> in December expelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>. The Marquis of Hamilton then advised Charles that war was the only course.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Charles's plan, to avoid recalling Parliament, of an English army from the south, a landing under Hamilton and an Irish force under the Earl of Antrim fell apart when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/covenanters">Covenanters</a> occupied Edinburgh Castle on 21 March 1639 and Dumbarton Castle, and Hamilton found the Forth strongly defended. The only significant fighting was in the north-east, where Montrose took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberdeen">Aberdeen</a> and defeated Viscount Aboyne at the Brig of Dee on 19 June. An English army of about 15,000, mostly untrained militia, met a Scottish force of 16,500 under Alexander Leslie near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berwick-upon-tweed">Berwick-upon-Tweed</a>. They negotiated and signed the Pacification of Berwick on 19 June.</p><p>Both sides saw it as a truce. The Scottish Parliament and the General Assembly confirmed the earlier decisions. Charles recalled the English Parliament in 1640, but the Short Parliament under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-pym">John Pym</a> demanded redress of grievances such as ship money and was dissolved after three weeks. In August 1640 Leslie's army of about 21,000 crossed the Tweed and won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-newburn">Battle of Newburn</a> on 28 August. Lord Conway withdrew to Durham and the Scots took Newcastle-upon-Tyne.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Under the truce of October 1640 the Scots were paid £850 a day and kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northumberland">Northumberland</a> and County Durham. Charles had to call the Long Parliament, which met in November 1640 and executed the Earl of Strafford in May 1641. The Treaty of London in August 1641 ended the wars and the Scots left northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. Political differences between the king and Parliament, sharpened by the Irish Rebellion of 1641, led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-english-civil-war">First English Civil War</a> in August 1642. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishops</a>' Wars were the first of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-the-three-kingdoms">Wars of the Three Kingdoms</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bishops' Wars” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It led to the <b>Bishops' Wars</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-28">28 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The resistance led to the National Covenant, the <b>Bishops' Wars</b> and the British civil wars.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-23">23 July in history</a></li></ul></section>
